# Branches: one context each, the area's branch first, main merged in, a squash back

## Pyramid Layer

- Layer: L0 convention.

## Use This When

- A fix or a feature is about to start and needs a branch.
- A change is about to be committed on a branch: it belongs to that branch's context.
- A branch is about to land on `main`.
- An experiment is about to start, or to end.
- A business commit of the UI contract is about to be written, or taken onto an area branch, or a branch has just landed on `main`: the contract trunk below.
- You are about to choose where to work: the main checkout or a worktree.
- An operation spans several branches (a reset, a delete, a rewrite, a sweep), or a branch is ready to push.

## Stop Here If

- The branch is chosen: the work already sits on its area's branch, with the latest `main` merged into it, and the branch holds nothing of another area. Landing it still follows the landing point below.

## Core rule

- **A branch is one context.** Branches keep the work's contexts apart: a branch holds one area's work, the responsibility its name and its card say, and its diff from `main` is the whole of that context. An experiment is the one exception (below): it may explore several contexts and carries its parent's unlanded work, while each of its commits keeps one destination.
  - The test is a thought experiment: export the branch alone, as an archive of what it changes, and hand it to someone who has only `main`. It stands on its own: what it changes, why (in the code, the docs and the tests it carries, never in a private source), and the proof (its own tests pass on it).
  - Nothing of another context rides along: an engine's branch does not change what an app does, and touches an app only where the app consumes what the engine changed (an import path, a call); a feature's branch carries no engine fix; a branch of rules carries no code.
  - A file belongs to the context of its responsibility, not of its folder: tooling of an engine found in an app's folder is the engine's, and moves into the engine on the engine's branch.
  - What a branch needs from another context lands first, on that context's own branch, and the branch merges `main` to get it; it never carries a copy or a cherry-pick of another branch's unlanded work, save an experiment's commits when the experiment is split and a card's business commits taken from the contract trunk (both below). A rule that describes how another branch's work is placed waits for that work to land too.
  - A change found along the way that belongs to another context goes to that context's branch and card, not into the one at hand.
- **Look for the area's branch before starting one.** An area is the responsibility a branch was opened for, the module, mechanism or feature its name and its card say, not a file it happened to touch. Work that continues an area a branch has already carried goes on that branch. The card of the work and the branch's name decide; the branches (`git branch -a`) and the `Squashed-from:` trailers of `main` on the files the work touches (`git log main --format='%h %s%n%b' -- <path>`) only propose candidates. Several candidates, or none that clearly owns the area: ask the developer before starting. Before reusing a branch, look at what it holds that `main` does not (`git log main..<branch>`): work still under way on another card is that card's, so ask before adding to it; work left unfinished or abandoned is the developer's call and never goes into the next squash unseen. Then merge the latest `main` into the branch, by a merge commit, never a rebase of what was pushed, and commit the fix or the feature on it. Apart from an experiment and the branch that carries its kept commits back to the parent (below), only an area no branch has carried gets a new branch, off `main`, named for the area and the kind of its work (`feat/<area>`, `chore/<area>`, `docs/<area>` and the like); it is that area's branch from then on. A `fix/<area>` branch is rare: it is only for an architectural fix, one that changes how the foundation is built. A defect, or a correction of a feature, goes on the branch of its area like any other work.
- **Land with one squash, keep the branch.** A branch lands on `main` as one squash commit, `type(scope): goal` and then the trailer `Squashed-from: <branch> (<tip>) <card>`, after the gates of `verify-before-commit-and-landing.mdc` and the developer's go. The branch stays: right after the landing, merge the squash commit itself back into it (`git merge <squash>`, not a `main` that has moved on since), then merge `main` into the contract trunk (below), and the developer pushes the branch and `main`. That merge is trivial, because the branch's work is already in the squash, and it makes the squash the branch's merge base with `main`, so the next round does not replay the squashed changes as conflicts; a newer `main` is then merged in the usual way. The next fix or feature of its area merges `main` into it again and lands as the next squash. Work that builds on another branch's work waits for that one to land, then merges `main` in.
- **An experiment returns into the branch it was cut from.** A pure experiment, work whose outcome is not decided yet, gets a branch named `experiment/<area>` (`experiment/flow-compression`), cut from the branch whose area it explores. It never lands on `main` itself: what is kept of the parent's area returns into the parent by a merge, and the parent lands as usual.
  - Every commit of an experiment can be cherry-picked on its own: it has one destination, one epic's branch, and one kind of commit (meta, business or implementation, `ui-contract.mdc`), never two epics or two kinds, and it builds only on commits that go to the same branch or to one that lands before it.
  - A later meta commit does not remove a form an earlier business commit of the experiment uses; if it has to, those business commits are written anew on the final engine when the experiment is split.
  - When the experiment ends, each kept commit goes to its one destination. When every commit of the experiment is kept and belongs to the parent, the experiment merges into it. Otherwise the kept commits of the parent's area are cherry-picked onto a branch cut from the parent, which then merges into it, and the kept commits of other epics are cherry-picked onto their own branches; each cherry-pick is committed through the pre-commit hook.
  - Once every kept commit has reached its destination, the experiment's branch is deleted, its tip tagged first (`archive/<area>`) when notes cite its hashes; the tag is a reference, not a branch to work on.
- **The contract trunk feeds the business layer to the area branches.** `contract/trunk` is the one context of the UI contract's business layer (`ui-contract.mdc`): `contract/domain/`, the content tables of `contract/vocabulary.md` and `tests/e2e/scenarios/`, nothing else. Cut from `main`, it lives in a worktree of its own (`mcproc worktree add`), kept as long as the trunk, for the developer's modeling session; it never lands and is never deleted. Its diff from `main` is the business layer designed and not yet built: `git log main..contract/trunk -- contract/domain contract/vocabulary.md tests/e2e/scenarios` lists it by commit, and `bun run contract:diff main` run on the trunk by screens, anchors, codes and actions.
  - Every commit of the trunk is a business commit of one card. A card may take several as its contract develops, each built only on its own card's commits and on those of cards that land before it. The card names its commits, oldest first, and records the developer's OK on its contract (`ui-contract.mdc`).
  - An area branch takes an approved card as one business commit: `git cherry-pick --no-commit <the card's commits, oldest first>`, then one `git commit` named for the card's goal, so the pre-commit hook checks it (a plain cherry-pick runs none, `verify-before-commit-and-landing.mdc`). The take is `main` plus that card's contract: where the card's lines sit next to another card's, the conflict keeps this card's lines and none of the other's. The direction is trunk to area only, and the card notes the take's hash beside the trunk's. A contract change the implementation needs is asked of the modeling session, committed on the trunk under the same card and taken the same way, as one more commit: the area branch's history is never rewritten. Only the last business commit of a rename or a removal is written on the area branch, once the code no longer renders the old names, since the trunk cannot drop a name `main` still renders.
  - The trunk takes `main` by a merge, never a rebase: as the first step of each modeling session, and as the last step of every landing, in the trunk's worktree while nothing in it is uncommitted (otherwise the modeling session's next start takes it). After the merge the trunk is `main` plus the cards not taken yet, and nothing else: a conflict in a business file keeps the trunk's lines of those cards, and `main`'s wherever `main` changed the business layer apart from the trunk (a rename's last commit, a meta round, an amendment written on an area branch); `bun run contract:diff main` then shows exactly that queue.
  - A feature whose business commits were already on its area branch when the trunk took the business layer finishes there, under the review stop of `ui-contract.mdc`.
- **Work in the main checkout; a worktree only for autonomous work or a busy checkout.** One activity runs in the main checkout, the one the developer's editor shows, so the developer sees each change where it is made. A worktree (`mcproc worktree add`) is only for relatively autonomous agent work, or for when the main checkout is busy: it has uncommitted changes, or a session is working in it right now. A clean main checkout on a branch that has landed, with no session at work there, is free, and switching it to the branch at hand is the first step. A worktree is removed (`mcproc worktree remove`, the branch stays) once its branch lands or is retired.
- **Touch only the branches of the work at hand; the developer pushes.** A branch rule applies from the day it is written: a branch that landed before it stays as it is (no merge-back is added to it afterwards), and its area, when it resumes, merges `main` into its branch as usual. An operation over the repository's other branches is listed and proposed to the developer, never run as a sweep. A destructive operation over several refs (a reset, a delete, a rewrite) first prints each ref with its current tip and its target, and runs on exactly that list. Before rewriting a branch's history, look at the remote (`git ls-remote origin <branch>`): a branch that was pushed is rewritten only on the developer's word. The remote is the developer's alone: an agent never pushes, force-pushes or deletes a remote branch, since every push and deletion moves the remote's statistics; it says what is ready to push, and the developer pushes it.
- **Example: a feature over the UI contract.** The profile's fixes needed a new facet of the contract's engine, so they took the three kinds of commit of `ui-contract.mdc` on two branches, the engine's and the profile's:
  1. The engine's area already had its branch, `feat/contract-first-pass`, landed before. The latest `main` was merged into it, the meta commits went on it (the engine learns data events and error fields, with the rules and the skill about the contract), and it landed as one squash.
  2. `fix/profile-ux`, the profile's branch, came off `main` after that landing. The business commit of the contract went first (the screen's flow regions and error fields, the scenarios: the review stop), then the implementation commits (the code and its tests), and it landed as one squash.
  The next change to the engine merges `main` into `feat/contract-first-pass` again, and the next fix of the profile merges `main` into `fix/profile-ux`. Written today, step 2's business commit is modelled on `contract/trunk` and taken from it as one commit, with no review stop on `fix/profile-ux`, and the landing ends with `main` merged into the trunk.
- **Example: a context that leaks.** Tooling of the contract's engine that sits in an app's folder, such as the walk that runs every flow in XState, makes every round on the engine's branch touch that app: exported alone, the engine's branch reads as a change to one app. The tooling moves into the engine, which runs it in its own check, and the app keeps what is its own, the test of its own machines. Likewise, a rule about branches is written on the branch of the branch rules, `chore/branch-flow`, not on the branch of the work that made it necessary.
